armdeck: let an in-game limiter apply Steam's frame limit

With ARMDECK_FPS_LIMIT_FILE set, gamescope stops enforcing Steam's frame limit
(no vblank divisor for the app's frames, no limited refresh cycle for the WSI
layer, and the limiter is not reported as engaged, so no client is switched to
FIFO) and writes the requested limit to that file in frames per second
(0 = no limit), computed like gamescope's own divisor. On the OnePlus 8 the
built-in limiter gave input delay, and half the requested rate with DXVK;
MangoHud's sleep-based fps_limit, fed from this file, gives a steady rate
without the delay. Without the variable, gamescope behaves as upstream.

--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -6715,10 +6715,42 @@
 	return dst;
 }
 
+// armdeck: with ARMDECK_FPS_LIMIT_FILE set, gamescope does not enforce Steam's frame limit itself.
+// Holding the app's frames for whole vblanks, and pacing the WSI layer (and DXVK) to the limited
+// refresh cycle, gave noticeable input delay and, with DXVK, half the requested frame rate on a
+// command-mode DSI panel. Instead the limit Steam asked for is written to that file, as frames
+// per second (0 = no limit, computed the same way as gamescope's own vblank divisor), and an
+// in-game, sleep-based limiter (MangoHud's fps_limit) applies it.
+static const char *armdeck_fps_limit_file()
+{
+	static const char *s_pszPath = getenv( "ARMDECK_FPS_LIMIT_FILE" );
+	return ( s_pszPath && *s_pszPath ) ? s_pszPath : nullptr;
+}
+
+static void armdeck_publish_fps_limit( float flFps )
+{
+	static float s_flLast = -1.0f;
+	const char *pszPath = armdeck_fps_limit_file();
+	if ( !pszPath || flFps == s_flLast )
+		return;
+	s_flLast = flFps;
+
+	std::string sTmp = std::string( pszPath ) + ".tmp";
+	FILE *pFile = fopen( sTmp.c_str(), "w" );
+	if ( !pFile )
+		return;
+	fprintf( pFile, "%.3f\n", flFps );
+	fclose( pFile );
+	rename( sTmp.c_str(), pszPath );
+}
+
 static void
 update_runtime_info()
 {
-	uint32_t limiter_enabled = g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS != 0 ? 1 : 0;
+	// armdeck: with an in-game limiter, the WSI layer, Mesa and DXVK are not told that gamescope's
+	// limiter is engaged: they would switch the game to FIFO, adding latency, and DXVK would
+	// recreate its swapchain at every change of the limit.
+	uint32_t limiter_enabled = g_nSteamCompMgrTargetFPS != 0 && !armdeck_fps_limit_file() ? 1 : 0;
 
 	// The file is a legacy fallback for clients without gamescope_limiter.
 	wlserver_set_frame_limiter_state( limiter_enabled );
